Make the class `final` (or all constructors private), make every field `private final`, set every field in the constructor, provide no setters, and — the step people forget — **defensively copy any mutable state on the way in and on the way out**. A `final List` field is not immutable; only the reference is fixed.

```java
public final class Order {
    private final List<Item> items;
    public Order(List<Item> items) { this.items = List.copyOf(items); }   // in
    public List<Item> items() { return items; }                            // already unmodifiable
}
```

It matters because an immutable object is automatically thread-safe: there is no state to see half-written, so it needs no synchronisation, can be freely shared and cached, and is safe as a `HashMap` key. Most concurrency bugs are mutable shared state, and immutability removes the category rather than guarding it.

Two bugs, one of them intermittent.

**The `equals` is broken.** It takes `Order` as the parameter type, so it *overloads* rather than overrides `Object.equals`. `HashMap` and `List.contains` call `equals(Object)` and will get the default reference comparison. The `@Override` annotation would have caught this at compile time, which is the real lesson.

**`hashCode` is missing.** Even with `equals` fixed, unequal hash codes put equal objects in different buckets, so map lookups miss.

The fix:

```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Order other)) return false;
    return Objects.equals(id, other.id);
}

@Override
public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id); }
```

And the better answer: if the class is a value carrier, make it a `record` and get both for free.
